"Offsets distribution curves along ordered baselines to compare shape across many groups.\n\nUse when: Many related distributions share a quantitative scale and meaningful ordering. Shape changes across groups matter more than exact pairwise density readings.\n\nAvoid when: Overlapping profiles would hide crucial tails or the groups have very small samples.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes the measured variable. Height above each group baseline encodes density; row position identifies the group.\n\nBuild: Estimate densities using a comparable bandwidth and normalization policy. Arrange group baselines by time, magnitude, or a documented domain order. Choose small overlap or separate rows and label baselines outside filled regions.\n\nCheck: Check all profiles share the same numeric domain. Verify area or height normalization is stated and consistent. Include sample sizes when group sizes differ substantially.\n\nPitfalls: Overlap is intrinsic to many ridgelines and can hide data; prefer nonoverlapping small multiples by default. Vertical offsets are layout positions and are not additional measured values.\n\nExample: Show synthetic hourly transaction-size distributions ordered from morning to evening, using lightly separated density ridges.\n\nAlternatives: Density plot, Violin plot, Lasagna plot."
